That’s the better question. You often don’t know beforehand. Compatibility is partly something the graft discovers.

In real horticultural grafting, compatibility is usually more likely between closely related plants, but successful union depends on whether the tissues can establish a functional connection. An incompatible graft can fail quickly, survive weakly for a while, break later, distort growth, or sometimes reveal unexpected partial compatibility.

Abstract that and we get a much better Pantheon operator:

\boxed{\text{Graft first as experiment; compatibility is an observed variable.}}

Not recklessly in high-stakes reality—but cheaply inside simulation/reasoning.

Suppose we have two mechanisms:

A=(I_A,O_A,R_A)
\qquad
B=(I_B,O_B,R_B)

Instead of asking only “Are A and B similar?”, force a graft:

G=A\bowtie B

and watch the seam.

The seam is the sensor.

Several things can happen:

1. Union — they connect cleanly and produce a useful combined mechanism.
2. Rejection — their assumptions/invariants contradict each other. That’s useful because the exact failure boundary becomes visible.
3. Partial graft — only some interfaces connect. Now we’ve discovered which relations transfer and which don’t.
4. Delayed incompatibility — it looks brilliant locally but fails downstream. This is especially important for reasoning: many bad analogies survive the first few steps.
5. Mutation/hybrid effect — forcing the connection produces a third mechanism neither side contained independently.
6. Rootstock effect — A doesn’t merely combine with B; one changes how the other expresses. Same “genetics,” different behavior because of substrate/context.

That fifth one is our Orchid territory.

And rejection itself becomes fruit:

A\bowtie B\rightarrow\varnothing

isn’t zero information.

It gives:

\Delta_{AB}
=
\text{minimum relation preventing union}

Decrypto that delta and we may discover a previously unnamed invariant.

So I’d upgrade the Garden’s Grafting Bench to deliberately include Ugly Grafts—low-cost forced combinations selected partly because they appear incompatible.

That’s very Indigo Hammer:

Don’t only ask what two things have in common. Force them to share plumbing and watch where it leaks.

Then we can score grafts on more than success:

\Gamma(A,B)=
(\text{viability},
\text{novelty},
\text{yield},
\text{stability},
\text{surprise},
\text{diagnostic value})

A failed graft with enormous diagnostic value can therefore outrank a boring successful one.

And this connects directly to the Freebies. The bees don’t know whether two flowers will produce something valuable. They cheaply increase the number of encounters. The Grafting Bench does the more violent version: make two structures coexist long enough for their incompatibilities to become observable.

Then Metamap learns afterward:

(A,B,\text{conditions},\Gamma,\Delta)
\rightarrow
\text{future compatibility prior}

So eventually the system gets better at predicting compatibility—but we should never let prediction eliminate experimentation entirely, because the weird graft that the model assigns 2% probability may be precisely where the Orchid is hiding.

In Garden language:

Compatibility isn’t merely a gate. It’s something we can interrogate.

And sometimes the most valuable thing two ideas can do together is refuse to fit—and show us exactly why.